+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Optional Ledger access
======================

This build uses the ${AGAVE_LEDGER_GROUP} group for Ledger access. Select its
members and enable the packaged hooks on each host that needs access.

Upgrading from manually installed hooks
---------------------------------------

If the group name matches your previous build, keep its membership. When
switching groups, unplug the Ledger before replacing the old helper and
verify that its node returns to root:wheel mode 0600. Set up the new group
as described below.

In /etc/hotplug/attach and /etc/hotplug/detach replace the old Ledger
block with this line before any exit that would skip it:

    ${TRUEPREFIX}/libexec/agave/ledger-access "${0##*/}" "$1" "$2"

Remove the old /etc/hotplug/ledger-access invocation and its Ledger-specific
argument handling, including any "set --" that appended --attach. Preserve
other local hotplug actions. For hooks dedicated entirely to the Ledger,
you can replace each whole script with the packaged example:

    example=${TRUEPREFIX}/share/examples/agave/ledger-hook
    doas install -o root -g wheel -m 0755 "$example" /etc/hotplug/attach
    doas install -o root -g wheel -m 0755 "$example" /etc/hotplug/detach

If /etc/rc.local calls /etc/hotplug/ledger-rescan, change that call to
${TRUEPREFIX}/libexec/agave/ledger-rescan. Proceed to verification below.
Future package upgrades deliver both helpers at these paths.

First-time setup
----------------

Run these commands from the desktop account, then log out and back in.

    doas groupadd ${AGAVE_LEDGER_GROUP} &&
        doas usermod -G ${AGAVE_LEDGER_GROUP} "$(id -un)"

If groupadd reports that the name already exists, check that the group is
dedicated to Ledger access before adding members or enabling the hooks.
If it serves another purpose, rebuild with a different AGAVE_LEDGER_GROUP.

For each missing hook, install the example as an executable script. For
an existing hook, add the invocation above alongside its local actions.

    example=${TRUEPREFIX}/share/examples/agave/ledger-hook
    for hook in /etc/hotplug/attach /etc/hotplug/detach; do
        if [ -e "$hook" ] || [ -L "$hook" ]; then
            echo "Existing $hook: integrate the Ledger invocation above"
        else
            doas install -o root -g wheel -m 0755 "$example" "$hook"
        fi
    done
    doas rcctl enable hotplugd
    doas rcctl start hotplugd

To reconcile existing nodes at boot, add
${TRUEPREFIX}/libexec/agave/ledger-rescan to /etc/rc.local. If a device
already attached at boot has no node, reconnect it once to trigger creation.
If you previously changed a Ledger node's owner or mode manually, restore
that specific node to root:wheel mode 0600 before enabling Ledger access.

Verification
------------

Check that "id -Gn" includes ${AGAVE_LEDGER_GROUP} and that
"rcctl check hotplugd" reports ok. Reconnect the Ledger, unlock it, and open its
Solana app. Check dmesg for its current uhid unit. For example, for uhid12:

    ls -li /dev/uhid12
    ${TRUEPREFIX}/bin/solana-keygen pubkey usb://ledger

A verified interface gets root:${AGAVE_LEDGER_GROUP} mode 0660. Run the
public-key check as your regular user. Unplug the Ledger and run ls again.
The same inode should remain with root:wheel mode 0600. Reconnect and check the
grant on the current unit. An attach event creates a missing node automatically.
